  would never need her. They thanked her for her service in a font too cheerful for the news.
  On her final night, a storm came in hard from the northeast. Rain hammered the glass. Mara lit the lamp the
  old way, though the new equipment hummed beside it, waiting.
  Around midnight, the power failed along the coast. The solar batteries, untested in winter, flickered and
  died. Only her lamp, fed by kerosene and wound by hand, kept turning, its beam sweeping the black water in
  slow, patient arcs.
  Then she saw it: a fishing boat, lights out, drifting toward the rocks below the point. Mara grabbed the
  brass signal lantern and flashed the old code her father had taught her. Turn south. Turn south. Turn south.
